\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru forex \highlight\uc2  looks set for record 2004  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Marcel Deza   \par \uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2004\par 16:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LIMA, \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jan 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which already this year has spent a third of what it did in the whole of 2003 on buying dollars to halt the sol's rise, looks set for a record year of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 Aggressive dollar purchases worth $317 million to date have brought the sol value down to 3.475 to the dollar, from around 3.45 in mid-January, its highest levels this year.   \par \par \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen in recent weeks on solid economic news, including a trade surplus, a rise in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and progress in the Camisea gas project which is set to come on stream in August.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said at the start of January that it expected to spend less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars this year than the $1.05 billion it did in 2003. Its record was $1.295 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1997.   \par \par \uc2 "It's clear that there's upward economic pressure on the sol and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to compensate for that, which makes it very likely that it will make a record amount (of dollar) purchases this year," Jorge Chavez, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, told Reuters.   \par \par \uc2 "If the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues, we could be seeing signs of a change in the (flotation regime) monetary policy, with a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that tries to fix the exchange rate, and that would be worrying," said Chavez, who heads the private Maximixe consultancy.   \par \par \uc2 Last year, the sol strengthened 1.2 percent against the dollar, compared with a fall of 2.4 percent in 2002. It ended 2003 at 3.461 against the dollar. Inflation was 2.48 percent last year.   \par \par \uc2 Some analysts said heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also push up interest rates for certificates.   \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's purchases have been completely balanced out by the issue of paper, but if it keeps \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it'll have to offer a better yield for investers since they could seek higher interest rates for certificates (of deposit) that are placed," said Cesar Medina, an analyst at Banco de Credito (CRE.LM).   \par \par \uc2 RATE FIXING?   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it is seeking to avoid brusque changes in the sol's price, not to fix the exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 But dealers are not so sure. "Although it denies it, it's fixing an exchange rate," said Medina.   \par \par \uc2 Other said it was awkward for them having the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  constantly stepping into the market.   \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  make us uncomfortable, it's turned into another competitor in the market, with the advantage that it has all the soles to (buy dollars and to) move prices, said one treasury official at a Lima bank who declined to be named.   \par \par \uc2 Pablo Secada, an analyst at the Peruvian Institute of Economy, said that as long as the country's economic fundamentals remained unchanged, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was entirely reasonable, even if it reduced banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  profits.   \par \par \uc2 "The bank has increased the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  position (of its reserves), it has more dollars to back up the soles which it issues, the government has given it more room to buy dollars and it is in a position to inject liquidity," Secada said.   \par \par \uc2 The bank's exchange position - which makes up part of its reserves - was $4.583 billion at the end of last year, compared with $3.341 billion at the end of 2002. It was $4.849 billion at January 20 this year.   \par \par \uc2 Peru has international reserves of $10.561 billion, the highest level since 1998.   \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should buy and sell dollars at the same time to make it harder for the banks who are speculating against the exchange rate," said Maximixe's Chavez.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers' forecasts for the sol's exchange rate this year vary between around 3.47 and 3.5 to the dollar. The 2004 budget forecasts a rate of 3.59 soles per dollar - a level that is virtually impossible to reach, analysts say.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040127e01r002yx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
