\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Peru's Sol Rock Steady In Oft-Turbulent Zone  \b0\par\par\uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2003\par 20:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Robert Kozak \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crashes are a common occurence, leading some countries to even throw in the towel to adopt the U.S. dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 But Peru is moving in the other direction.  \par \par \uc2 Its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol, has shown remarkable stability despite political turbulence that has driven down President Alejandro Toledo's popularity ratings.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, authorities are aiming to reduce the nation's heavy reliance on the greenback for buying goods that are seen as a store of value, such as houses.  \par \par \uc2 Peru's free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained at about 3.5 soles per dollar in the last few years, while so far this year it has appreciated, gaining a nominal accumulated 1.04%, the Central Reserve Bank of Peru said.  \par \par \uc2 But a number of factors, including rising copper and gold prices, are putting so much pressure on the sol that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been forced to buy U.S. dollars on an almost daily basis.  \par \par \uc2 Curiously for a country that a little more than decade ago was rent by hyperinflation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now more concerned about consumer prices weakening too much.  \par \par \uc2 "A big part of the recent stability is tied to the participation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said Banco Wiese Sudameris economist Mario Guerrero.  \par \par \uc2 "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hadn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and had let the sol gain, inflation would be much lower," he added.  \par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at 3.4811 per dollar on Monday, after the Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy U.S. dollars.  \par \par \uc2 So far this year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped into the market to buy $$825.7 million on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. That's the largest amount since 1997, when a boom in the economy led to large-scale inflows of foreign capital.  \par \par \uc2 Most of the purchases have been from $3 million to $15 million a day, large enough to sway the small \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par            Trade Surpluses Help \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 As the mining sector pumps out more minerals, Peru has started to post trade surpluses. That, alongside shifts into sol-denominated portfolio instruments, has helped dampen expectations of a depreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although gross domestic product is seen expanding about 4.0% this year, consumer prices pressures remain weak.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So weak that observers say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is concerned a stronger sol will propel consumer prices even lower, and push inflation outside the band it has established.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has moved to a system of inflation targeting, set at 2.5% within a one percentage point band, for this year and into the near future. Year-over-year inflation of was 1.3% in October.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Observers say inflation could come in at the lower end of the band this year, or even lower.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will have problems having inflation remains within its range as private consumption is decelerating and there are strong pressures for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to appreciate," BBVA Banco Continental said in a report.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monetary authorities say they have no target for the sol, but are \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to "smooth out" its appreciation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, in a recent review of Peru's economy, the International Monetary Fund, saying a floating exchange rate regime has helped the country adjust to external shocks, also cautioned against too much \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In line with the authorities' announced policy, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market should be limited to smoothing excessive volatility in the exchange rate," the IMF said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moreover, officials hope the sol's stability will help wean the nation from its heavy reliance on the U.S. dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many Peruvian have bad memories of the hyperinflation in the late 1980s and early 1990s that robbed the value of their savings. The government of former President Alan Garcia even introduced complicated multiple exchange rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That drove many into the arms of the U.S. dollar, which is legal tender in Peru and which makes up about 80% of all bank loans.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The IMF, in its report, noted that the high degree of financial dollarization while declining, "remains a source of vulnerability."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Robert Kozak is bureau chief in Lima for Dow Jones Newswires)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires;511-221-7050; peru@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020031111dzbb0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
